 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 19,
   "y": 3,
   "text": "NORTH: Bushmills, the Causeway and the north coast. The river goes that way too, if it can be said to be in a hurry."
  },
  {
   "x": 7,
   "y": 44,
   "text": "SOUTH: Lough Neagh and Toome. The biggest lake in these islands is in the way, and you'll have to go round it."
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 4,
   "text": "THE DARK HEDGES. An avenue of beech trees planted in the 1770s, to impress visitors coming up to the big house. Two hundred and some years on, they still do, and the photographers know it."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 38,
   "text": "THE LOWER BANN. The river leaves Lough Neagh at Toome and makes its way to the sea at Coleraine, about thirty-six miles of slow brown water. The Bann Way follows the bank, mostly."
  },
  {
   "x": 45,
   "y": 24,
   "plaque": "lower_bann_dark_hedges",
   "title": "The Dark Hedges",
   "text": "The Dark Hedges are an avenue of beech trees on the Bregagh Road near Armoy, planted in the 1700s by the Stuart family as an approach to their house. Around ninety trees remain, their branches knitted overhead so that in some lights the road becomes a tunnel. They have been in films and television programmes, and some of the trees have been lost to storms over the years."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 13,
   "plaque": "lower_bann_mount_sandel",
   "title": "Mount Sandel",
   "text": "A short way up the Bann above Coleraine is Mount Sandel, where hunters and fishers made camp around 7000 BC. It is among the oldest known sites of human settlement in Ireland. They lived in huts of bent saplings and ate salmon, eels, hazelnuts and pig, which, when you think about it, is a perfectly good menu."
  },
  {
   "x": 16,
   "y": 37,
   "plaque": "lower_bann_neagh",
   "title": "Lough Neagh and the Giant",
   "text": "Lough Neagh is the largest lake in the British Isles, about 150 square miles of it. The legend says that the giant Finn McCool, in a quarrel with a Scottish rival, tore up a lump of ground and threw it across the sea. It missed, and fell into the Irish Sea, and became the Isle of Man. The hole it left filled with water, and the lake has been very proud of the arrangement ever since."
  }
 ],
